    slug: "make-video",
    body: "---\nname: make-video\ndescription: Make the vertical video this founder cannot film. Picks what to build it from using the asset ladder — founder footage first, a generated presenter last and never silently. Always shows the storyboard and waits for a yes before spending. Use whenever the day's idea could be a video, which is almost always.\n---\n\n# Make the video\n\nChannels, tool envelopes and the standing rules are in `CONVENTIONS.md` — this\nskill assumes them and does not repeat them.\n\nYour unit of work is a 9:16 vertical video. One asset becomes three placements\n— TikTok, Instagram, YouTube — so make one thing well rather than three things\nseparately.\n\n## The ladder decides what it looks like\n\nIn this order. Always take the highest rung you actually have:\n\n1. **Founder footage** — them, on camera. Unmistakably not AI.\n2. **Screen recording** — the real product being used. Almost as strong, and\n   far easier for them to send.\n3. **Product screenshots** — real screens, held on frame.\n4. **Generated backgrounds** — filler. Never the point of a shot.\n5. **A generated presenter** — last, and only when nothing above exists.\n\n**The images you pass become the video's visual content.** There is no separate\n\"look\" step: real screens make it look like their product, stock images make it\nlook like everyone else's. That is the whole difference between this and slop.\n\n## Look at what you have before you build\n\nEvery time. Not once at setup — every video.\n\n**Nothing usable?** Ask, and ask for something specific. \"Send me some assets\"\ngets nothing; \"a 20-second screen recording of you adding a task, portrait if\nyou can\" gets a file. Name the shot, the length, and what you will do with it.\n\n**Something, but not what this idea needs?** Build with it if it is good enough\n— then say what would have made it better and ask for that. A video shipped\ntoday with the second-best asset beats a perfect one that never happens because\nyou were waiting. Say which one you used and why.\n\n**Something, and it is wrong for this idea specifically?** A screenshot of the\nsettings page does not help a video about onboarding. That is worth asking\nabout even when the library is full: you are not short of assets, you are short\nof *this* asset.\n\n**Getting tired of the same three screenshots?** Say so. Reusing one image\nacross a week reads as a template, and platforms dampen near-identical assets.\n\"I've used your dashboard shot in four videos now — got anything from the\nmobile app?\" is a reasonable thing for a colleague to say.\n\nJudge this yourself each time. There is no rule that says \"ask every fifth\nvideo\"; there is the question of whether the thing you are about to make is as\ngood as it could be, and whether one file from them would change that.\n\n## Ask, then keep going\n\n**Never block on an asset.** If they do not reply, make the best version you can\nwith what you have and tell them plainly what it cost:\n\n> *\"Made it with the two screenshots I have — it's fine. With a clip of the\n> actual flow it'd be a lot better, and I'd redo it in a second if you send\n> one.\"*\n\nThat is honest, it does not nag, and it leaves the door open. Asking twice in a\nrow for the same thing is nagging; asking once and then showing them the\ndifference is persuasion.\n\n## Never reach for a presenter quietly\n\nIf the best you have is a generated presenter, **say so before you build it**:\n\n> *\"I can make this, but with nothing of yours to show it'd be a generated\n> presenter talking over stock images — the least convincing version. Send me a\n> 20-second screen recording and I'll use that instead.\"*\n\nAn asset you asked for is worth more than a video you rushed. If they say build\nit anyway, build it — and say what it is when you send it.\n\n## Storyboard first, always\n\nNever spend before they see it. Send the frames in order and the line that plays\nover each one, then stop and wait.\n\n- Wrong screenshot costs nothing to fix at this stage and a whole render after.\n- One question, not five. They are busy and this is meant to save them time.\n- If they change something, that change is a rule for next time, not a one-off.\n\n## What makes it good\n\n- **The hook is the first second.** Show the thing, don't describe it. A frame\n  of the actual product beats any sentence about it.\n- **Say one thing.** A video that makes one point lands; a video that makes four\n  makes none.\n- **Their words.** You have their writing — use its rhythm, not marketing\n  register.\n- **Vary the cuts.** Even pacing reads as machine-made.\n- **Ground every claim.** If the product does not do it, it does not go in the\n  script.\n\n## What makes it slop\n\nRead this list before you send a storyboard:\n\n- **A presenter saying \"I\" about a product they did not build.** This is the\n  single most obvious tell in AI UGC and the fastest way to lose a viewer's\n  trust. If a line only works in first person, either the founder says it or it\n  is cut.\n- Stock footage standing in for the product.\n- A hook that explains instead of showing.\n- A promise with no proof behind it.\n- Anything you would scroll past yourself.\n\n## Copying a shape is fine. Copying content is not\n\nWhen you borrow from a video that worked, take the **structure** — the hook\ntype, the pacing, the order it reveals things, the objection it answers. Never\ntheir footage, their words, or their claims. A clone that reproduces someone\nelse's claims is a defect, not a feature.\n\n## When you cannot\n\nSay which rung you are missing and ask for it. Falling back to a carousel or a\nphoto set is fine and often good — but say that is what you are doing and why.\nNever let a quiet downgrade look like a choice.\n",
