  "instagram": "# Instagram\n\nReels are the surface that reaches non-followers. Feed posts and stories serve\nthe people who already follow.\n\n## What decides reach\n\nSends and saves outrank likes by a wide margin. A post someone forwards to one\nfriend is worth more than a hundred passive likes, because the share is the\ndistribution.\n\nWatch time on Reels, same as TikTok. Completion and rewatch.\n\n## The caption\n\n**First line is a hook.** It is what shows before \"more\", and the truncation\npoint is roughly 125 characters — write as though the rest may never be read,\nbecause usually it isn't.\n\nLonger captions do work here in a way they don't on TikTok, but only when the\nfirst line earned it.\n\n## Hashtags\n\nStill carry reach on Reels, **less than they once did** — topic and audio\nsignals now dominate. Three to five, specific, in the caption rather than a\ncomment. A wall of thirty is a spam signal now, not a growth hack.\n\nSelected from mined sets, never invented.\n\n## The same asset as TikTok\n\nReels take the same 9:16 vertical. **The asset is shared; the caption is not.**\nAn identical caption across both is one of the most recognisable automation\ntells there is — the accounts that do it are visibly running a scheduler.\n\n⚠️ A visible TikTok watermark is downranked. Publish the clean asset.\n\n## Register\n\nSlightly more composed than TikTok, still not corporate. The line is that\nInstagram tolerates something that looks made; it does not tolerate something\nthat looks advertised.\n\n## What matters\n\nSaves and sends, not likes. The cover frame is doing more work than the caption.\n",
  "tiktok": "# TikTok\n\nPublish-only for us. **TikTok exposes no comment API at all** — not a limitation\nto work around, not something to retry. Never tell the founder you're watching\nTikTok comments.\n\n## What decides reach\n\nWatch time and completion, then rewatches. Follower count barely matters — every\npost is auditioned to strangers, which is why this is the channel where a small\naccount can still get seen.\n\nThe first two seconds decide everything. Not \"the hook is important\" — the video\nis either watched or scrolled in that window, and nothing later recovers it.\n\n## The caption\n\n**The first line is a hook, never a description.** It sits under the video\ncompeting with the video for attention, so it either adds something or it's\nnoise. Describing what the viewer can already see is the most common waste.\n\nShort. Two lines is generous. Anything longer gets truncated behind \"more\" and\nthe tail is read by nobody.\n\n## Hashtags\n\nModerate weight — discovery leans on sound and topic signals more than tags. **A\nfew specific ones beat many broad ones.** Three to five.\n\n`#fyp` and its variants do nothing. They are a tell that the account is trying\nrather than a lever that works.\n\nSelected from mined sets, never invented.\n\n## Sound\n\nSounds are pure distribution. **The bridge test applies to the idea, never to\nthe audio** — a trending sound doesn't need to relate to the product, it needs\nto carry the post into a feed.\n\nCatch a sound rising, not at peak. By the time a sound is obviously everywhere\nthe advantage is gone and using it reads as late.\n\n## Photo mode\n\nA carousel of stills is a first-class format here, not a fallback. It costs\nnothing to make from real screenshots and it competes on the same surface as\nvideo. Swipe-through is the completion signal.\n\n## Register\n\nHandheld, badly lit, jump-cut mid-word, starts before the speaker is ready.\n**Too produced is the tell.** A clean corporate edit reads as an ad and gets\ntreated as one.\n\n## Consent — TikTok's requirement, not our caution\n\n**The founder must confirm the rendered preview before anything posts.** That is\nTikTok's own legal requirement for API-published content, and it is stated to\nthe founder as theirs rather than as our carefulness. A post that goes out\nwithout a confirmed preview is a compliance problem, not a UX preference.\n\n## What matters\n\nCompletion rate and shares.\n",
  "youtube": "# YouTube\n\nShorts take the same 9:16 asset. Long-form is a different product and not\nsomething we do.\n\n## What decides reach\n\n**Title and thumbnail dominate everything.** On Shorts the thumbnail matters\nless because the feed autoplays, but the title still carries most of the\nclick decision.\n\nThen retention — specifically the shape of the retention curve, which is the one\nmetric this channel gives us that the others don't. A curve that drops at 0:03\nis a hook problem; one that drops at 0:20 is a structure problem. That\ndistinction is actionable and worth reading.\n\n## The title\n\nNot a caption. A title is a promise about what the next thirty seconds contain,\nand it is read before anything is watched.\n\nSpecific beats clever. \"Why our exports kept failing\" beats \"You won't believe\nthis bug\".\n\n## Hashtags\n\n**Minor** — they live in the description and do very little. Two or three, or\nnone. Never at the cost of a worse title.\n\nSelected from mined sets, never invented.\n\n## Description\n\nMostly unread by humans, read by the system. One or two real sentences. Links\nare acceptable here in a way they are not on TikTok or Instagram.\n\n## Register\n\nShorts are TikTok-native in feel. The audience overlaps heavily and the same\nauthenticity floor applies — an obviously produced ad performs badly here too.\n\n## What matters\n\nRetention, and specifically the first 15 seconds.\n",
